# Event-Time Backtest Standard

When evaluating any trading strategy in this project:

- Never judge validity only from fixed checkpoints such as 20-day or 60-day returns.
- Define D explicitly as the signal-confirmation close. Use the next session open as the primary executable entry.
- Produce the complete D+1 through D+100 path when data permits. If later horizons are censored, show the sample count at every D+N.
- Report daily mean, median, positive-return rate, quartiles, and same-date universe-relative excess return.
- Include +10% and +15% target hit rates, median days to target, MFE, MAE, and drawdown over the path.
- Keep signal-close descriptive returns separate from next-open executable returns.
- Use only information available at D. Label any hindsight-defined bottom separately and never treat it as a tradable signal.
- De-duplicate persistent signals into episodes and report both raw-event and one-position-per-ticker results.
- Attach QQQ MA200 distance, regime, QQQ RSI/MACD, VIX, and available market breadth at D.
- Compare variants and existing strategies under identical entry, cost, holding, and exit assumptions.
- Include chronological out-of-sample or early/late stability checks and ticker/year concentration.
- State price source, adjustment method, fees, survivorship bias, and unavailable data.
- Fixed 20/60-day metrics may be shown as checkpoints, but never as the sole or primary evidence.
